WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.480
So as usual, let's start
from our classic roadmap

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.160
just to have an exact point
of where we are right now.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.920
So over the last week or
so, we started approaching

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.180
this quadrant of the roadmap.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:22.240
Specifically, we started
looking at open loop

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.360
methods for optimal control.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.640
Last Monday, we started
looking at indirect methods

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.200
for optimal control.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.840
And we'll spend also today in
this box over here and Monday.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.720
Then next week Wednesday
we'll move to direct methods

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:40.260
for open loop optimal control.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.360
And then we'll move
towards closed loop

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.440
methods in the following weeks.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.920
And I just want to
make sure that we have

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.620
the key takeaways in place.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.940
And specifically, when on Monday
we introduced indirect methods,

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.240
we essentially said
that indirect methods

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.920
are a way of defining
necessary optimality conditions

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.190
for optimal control problems
in their infinite dimensional

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:04.709
optimization case.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:09.990
And specifically, again, what
I mean by infinite dimensional

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:18.950
is that we want to optimize
with over functionals, which

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.790
are in and of itself,
loosely speaking,

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.890
defined as functions
over functions.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:29.230
So we want to find
the optimal control u

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:32.910
star of t that optimizes a given
functional that is given to us.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:36.710
And again, u of t is a
continuous signal in the sense

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.240
that it is defined between
some time t0 and tf.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:41.990
And again, loosely
speaking, it represents

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:46.830
to optimizing the full
control signal over time.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:50.210
And in order to derive these
necessary optimality conditions,

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.910
we had to extend
basically everything

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:58.220
we know from the domain of
finite dimensional optimization.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.460
And essentially
derived a key result,

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.840
which is the fundamental theorem
of calculus of variation.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.360
So these slides are not the
ones you will find online.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.220
These are just some selected
slides from Monday just

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.060
to recap.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.740
And the key result is this
fundamental theorem of calculus

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:20.340
of variations, which essentially
says that for a function x star

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.980
to be an extremal--
so loosely speaking,

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.300
a local minimum or optimum
for our functional--

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:32.540
the variation of j
must vanish at x star.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:35.020
Namely, we derive
this equation here

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.980
that the variation of j, of
our cost function j evaluated

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.580
at the optimal x
star needs to be

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.660
0 for all admissible
variations delta x.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.860
And in your mental map,
something that I want to stress

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.580
is that this condition
here, when we map it

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:58.050
to the finite dimensional
case, should essentially

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:02.370
feel as if we are saying that
the differential is equal to 0.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:08.770
So that the gradient of f
transpose delta x is equal to 0.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.890
Now this result is
obviously important

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.010
because it characterizes
what is a local minimum

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.950
in our case in the case of
infinite dimensional functions.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.650
But it is not actionable.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:22.690
So what we did on Monday is that
we took a specific, simplified

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:25.170
optimal control problem,
which is essentially

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:28.930
the one where we were assuming
to have a functional defined

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:33.690
through this stagewise cost g,
which is a function of x of t,

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.610
x dot of t and time.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.250
And so essentially
speaking, here we

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:41.510
refer to it as simplified
because if it's as is,

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.870
we are directly controlling
the state trajectory.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:47.170
So no explicit controls
you in this case.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.570
And we were assuming
and we essentially

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:55.120
derived from the fundamental
theorem of calculus of variation

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.120
an actionable strategy
to solve these problems

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:02.097
and to define necessary
optimality conditions.

00:04:02.097 --> 00:04:03.680
And of course, we
did this in the case

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.143
where both the initial and
final conditions were fixed.

00:04:07.143 --> 00:04:09.060
So the initial time and
final time were fixed.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.960
And the initial state x
and final state x at tf

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.120
is also fixed.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:17.120
And so we started if you
remember, a derivation where

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.260
we took the increment of the
functional we linearized.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.460
So we took the linear
part of that increment.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:25.400
And then we derived a
condition on that linear part

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.860
of the increment, which boiled
down to the Euler equation.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:32.520
Essentially here is where we
fast tracked Newton in just one

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.920
lecture essentially.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:37.600
And here is where I want to
now delve a little bit deeper

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.460
because we didn't
have time on Monday,

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:43.320
is that this Euler equation is,
again, in your mental model,

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:48.520
you should essentially
see the Euler equation

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.920
as the analog of
the gradient of f

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:54.920
equal to 0 condition in
the finite dimensional case

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.560
because now this is actionable.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.440
We can actually compute this.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.400
Now let's look a
little bit closer

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.960
at what this Euler
equation looks like.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.460
It is a nonlinear ordinary
differential equation,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.860
specifically a second order
differential equation.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:16.200
Because here we have a function
g of x dot that is also

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:21.280
is differentiated with respect
to time once more here.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:22.920
And specifically,
it is an equation

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:26.200
with split boundary conditions.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.760
So now you might be more
familiar with the type

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.280
of differential equations,
which are regarded

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.040
as in the form of
Cauchy problems

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:40.740
where usually the boundary
conditions are only one-sided,

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.840
for example, at
the initial time 0.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.980
But here is, if you wish, this
is a slightly nastier object,

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.418
because in this case, the
boundary conditions are split.

00:05:49.418 --> 00:05:50.960
So we have them both
at the beginning

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.210
and at the end of
time in this case.

00:05:58.516 --> 00:06:00.690
Any questions on this point?

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:04.970
Yes.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:07.310
Can you explain
why it's OK-- why

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:12.910
you have x dot instead of
an optimal control sequence?

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:16.310
Yeah, so this is derived on
this simplified setting where

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:20.150
we are assuming that we can
control the dynamics, if you

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:22.110
wish, as if we're
directly controlling

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.150
the state trajectory.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.990
In the next slides, we will
relax some of these assumptions

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:31.550
and build towards an actual
optimal control problem.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.430
So what we'll do now is I'll
spend maybe five minutes

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:36.390
or so doing a concrete
example of how

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:39.830
to use these optimality
conditions in practice.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:41.910
And then we'll move
towards relaxing

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:45.150
these boundary conditions and
these simplifying assumptions

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:48.070
that we just introduced here.

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:51.740
And specifically, the
problem we'll be looking at

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:57.420
is one of finding
the minimum shortest

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.600
path between two points.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.500
So even just to
build a mental map,

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:23.160
assume we are at a specific
time 0 and x sub 0.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.020
We want to find the
shortest line that

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:29.980
connects these two points.

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:33.060
And it will give us
a great satisfaction

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.180
in deriving the fact that this
is actually the straight line.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.040
And the way we will
do this, of course,

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.020
is by leveraging
calculus of variation.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:46.760
So remember that, first of
all, to approach this problem,

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:49.530
we have to define what
is a functional that

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:53.570
represents the minimum distance
between these two points.

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:58.410
And remember from
your calculus classes

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:02.510
that in order to measure
the length of a line,

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:08.490
what we do in practice is that
we take a infinitesimal segment

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:11.850
ds, and then we integrate
it over the entire length

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:12.850
of the line.

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:17.690
And so for us we will
define the-- so the length

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:20.530
of the segment is something
along the lines of taking

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:23.690
this infinitesimal displacement
and just integrating it

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.970
over the length of the line.

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:29.510
Now, how do we calculate
this in practice?

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:34.570
As usual, we are often only
good at handling linear objects.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:39.730
And so assuming that
the s is small enough,

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:43.850
we do this by taking an
infinitesimal displacement

00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:49.160
in time, which I'm calling dt,
and in the x direction, which

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:50.480
I'm calling the dt.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.340
And so from using a
Pythagorean theorem,

00:08:54.340 --> 00:09:03.280
we can approximate ds as ds
squared equals dt squared

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:06.140
plus the delta x squared.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:13.680
OK, from this, we
can, of course,

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.600
derive the fact that delta s is
equal to the square root of dt

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:23.440
squared plus delta x squared.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.420
Taking delta t out
of the square root,

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.560
we would get that delta t
multiplies the square root of 1

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:36.020
plus delta x over
delta t squared.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:42.840
And assuming there are no
mathematicians in this class,

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:48.030
here we are redefining
this as x dot.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:53.470
And so this leads to our
definition of the functional j

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:57.990
as the integral between
t0 and tf of our equation

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:01.510
that we just derived for
ds, which is essentially

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:10.670
the square root of 1 plus x
dot of t squared in the t.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:15.930
And this is essentially
our g of x x dot.

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:20.470
Yeah.

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:27.370
So now to solve this, we
want to be methodical.

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:29.930
So we want to apply
a Euler equation.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:33.550
So our necessary
optimality conditions.

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:38.310
So to do this, what we need is
to first of all compute g of x

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:44.260
and g of x dot, which
as a reminder, so g of x

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:48.020
is equal to the derivative
of g with respect

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:54.520
to x, which in this case,
the g does not depend on x.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:59.660
It only depends on x dot,
and so this is equal to 0.

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:02.260
The next thing that
we need is indeed

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:16.900
to define g of x dot,
which is 1 over 2

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:20.740
multiplied by square
root of 1 plus x dot

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:27.900
squared times 2 times x dot.

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:29.740
The 2 cancels out.

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:32.540
And so for us g of
x dot is essentially

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:42.570
x dot times 1 plus x dot squared
power of the negative 1 over 2.

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:45.930
So here we're just
basically computing

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:47.930
those terms that we
need for in order

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:49.710
to apply the Euler equation.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:12:00.370
So now what we want is indeed
just computing that and spelling

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:01.890
out that Euler equation.

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:05.310
So what we need is to
compute the derivative.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:07.850
So we know that the
first term is 0.

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:11.730
And so what we need is the
derivative of g of x dot with

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:19.930
respect to time, which is equal
to x double dot times 1 plus x

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:25.570
dot squared to the power
of negative 1 over 2 plus

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:29.210
the derivative of
the second term,

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:39.010
which is indeed x dot multiplied
negative 1/2 times 1 plus x dot

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:44.960
squared to the power of
negative 3 over 2 multiplied

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:47.620
by the derivative
of the term inside,

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:52.520
which is 2x dot x double dot.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:55.140
Again, just some
derivations here.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:05.200
Now we can rewrite this as x
double dot over 1 plus x dot

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:15.320
squared to the power of 1/2
minus x dot squared times x

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:25.400
double dot over 1 plus x dot
squared to the power 3 over 2,

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:32.640
which we can rewrite as x double
dot plus x double dot times x

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:38.200
dot squared minus x dot
squared x double dot.

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:42.430
Or everything over
1 plus x dot squared

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:47.550
to the power of 3
over 2, which finally

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:53.030
is equal to x double dot
over 1 plus x dot squared

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:54.650
to the power of 3 over 2.

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:10.310
So now basically, what we have
is we have both of the terms

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:13.790
that we need for
our Euler equation.

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:18.870
And essentially, we can then
write our necessary optimality

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.910
conditions as the fact that--

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:25.790
so g of x is equal to 0.

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:29.830
And so we can write
that x double dot has

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:38.710
to be over 1 plus x dot
squared to the power 3 over 2

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:42.270
needs to be equal to 0.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:45.790
Now we know that this
is always positive.

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:50.830
And so the only way for
this term to be equal to 0

00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:53.970
is that x double dot
has to be equal to 0.

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:58.910
And so we essentially
reach that this is now

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:06.850
our Euler equation fully fleshed
out, which means, of course,

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:14.870
our boundary conditions, which
in our case are that x of t0

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:20.550
is equal to x0 and x
of tf are equal to xf.

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:24.470
Again, here we didn't really
specify what x0 and xf are,

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:28.510
but this is what the boundary
conditions look like.

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:30.930
Now, this is something
we know how to solve.

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:42.500
So what is the solution
here when we integrate

00:15:42.500 --> 00:15:44.420
x double dot equals 0?

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:45.140
Yes.

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:47.940
It's a straight line since
there's no [INAUDIBLE]?

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:48.680
Yeah, exactly.

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:52.340
So writing it down,
it means that x of t

00:15:52.340 --> 00:16:00.743
is equal to c1t plus C2, which
it is indeed the-- basically,

00:16:00.743 --> 00:16:01.660
the formula of a line.

00:16:01.660 --> 00:16:04.940
The equation of a line.

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:08.322
Now notice that
here we have, again,

00:16:08.322 --> 00:16:09.780
pointing your
attention to the fact

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:12.300
that this is a second-order
differential equation.

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:16.100
This will need two
constants of integration

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:17.520
that we need to pinpoint.

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:20.020
And so here we're using
our two boundary conditions

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:25.140
to pinpoint those
constants of integration.

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:29.580
And so we indeed proved that the
shortest path between two points

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:31.060
is a line.

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:33.540
Now formally, what
we proved here

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:37.330
is that the line is a
candidate for optimality.

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:40.890
There are second order
arguments could be done here

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:43.790
to actually prove that
this is an actual optimum,

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:46.210
but this is the way
mechanically in which

00:16:46.210 --> 00:16:48.150
we would use these conditions.

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:02.770
Now if this is
clear, I would move

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:05.290
towards what is probably
the most interesting for us

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:07.210
and for the purpose
of this class, which

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:10.690
is wanting to now
apply these ideas

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:12.869
towards the optimal
control problem.

00:17:12.869 --> 00:17:16.170
And to do this, we need
to expand and relax

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:19.430
some of these assumptions we're
doing to simplify the setup.

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:19.930
Yes.

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:26.010
So what you just did again,
this x dot is 0 under condition.

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:27.770
Was that an x double dot?

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:28.329
What?

00:17:28.329 --> 00:17:28.873
Sorry.

00:17:28.873 --> 00:17:29.790
Oh, it's a double dot.

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:30.590
This is double dot.

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:31.090
Yes.

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:40.560
Ali made sure that the markers
are all nice and working today.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.200
Didn't you just integrate it?

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:43.680
So once you have
the x double dot

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:45.760
is 0, literally
just [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:46.920
Yeah.

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:49.815
Fair enough.

00:17:49.815 --> 00:17:54.080
All right, so this
is the mechanics

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:57.560
of how you would use the
Euler equation, essentially

00:17:57.560 --> 00:18:00.500
the optimality conditions for
the case we're looking at.

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:03.160
And now we want to build towards
problems that we actually

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:05.680
care about solving, which
are optimal control problems.

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:09.920
And so the first extension that
we want to do to this formalism

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:13.240
is relaxing the assumptions
that the boundary conditions

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:14.140
are fixed.

00:18:14.140 --> 00:18:15.920
So we derived all of
this using the fact

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:19.420
that initial time, final
time, initial state condition,

00:18:19.420 --> 00:18:21.640
a final condition were fixed.

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:23.660
And so we want to
relax this assumption.

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:27.400
And so what we do here is we
take the exact same setup.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:29.520
Now formally, we're
considering functions

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:34.030
which map reals to reals in Rn.

00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:35.910
So we're just like a
multidimensional version

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:36.410
of that.

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:37.890
But it shouldn't bother you.

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:41.615
It's exactly the same thing.

00:18:41.615 --> 00:18:42.990
With the usual
assumptions, we're

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:46.070
assuming that g is
twice differentiable.

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:49.730
The initial conditions
t0 and x of 0 are fixed.

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:55.150
But now we are relaxing the fact
that the final time and x of tf

00:18:55.150 --> 00:18:58.830
might be fixed or free.

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:05.070
And so essentially,
what we want to do here

00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:09.550
is adjust our
optimality conditions

00:19:09.550 --> 00:19:12.590
in order to handle these cases.

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:15.250
Now the problem here, as
you can probably imagine,

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:17.790
is that-- so we showed here
already on this example

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:19.830
that the optimality
conditions boils down

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:23.110
to solving these
differential equations.

00:19:23.110 --> 00:19:25.990
In order to solve them,
we need to pinpoint

00:19:25.990 --> 00:19:28.190
some constants of integration.

00:19:28.190 --> 00:19:31.540
And here we use the fact
that we had these boundary

00:19:31.540 --> 00:19:34.720
conditions fixed to pinpoint
those constants of integrations.

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:36.280
But the moment we
are relaxing this,

00:19:36.280 --> 00:19:38.580
we are missing some
boundary conditions.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:43.260
So essentially what happens
here is that the Euler equation

00:19:43.260 --> 00:19:45.480
remains valid.

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:46.960
So it still has to hold.

00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:49.220
But now we are lacking
these boundary conditions

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:51.940
to pinpoint the
constants of integration.

00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:55.580
And so this is in
general, for today,

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:59.380
I will be limiting myself
to stating the results.

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:01.280
We will be proving
also some of them.

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:03.500
But most of the
proofs are one, very

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:06.880
similar to the manipulations
we did on Monday.

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:09.180
And so not very
insightful in itself.

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:11.140
And so I will focus
on stating the results

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:14.220
and showing you how to use them.

00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:16.180
And so the fact
here is that we need

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:19.220
to have a more sophisticated
version of the boundary

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:20.980
conditions.

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:23.220
And specifically the
boundary conditions

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:25.940
are found from
this equation here,

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:29.130
which, at the first approach
might seem like a very

00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:30.910
nasty equation to deal with.

00:20:30.910 --> 00:20:34.730
So let me unpack it and
show you how to use it.

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:36.730
So first of all, notice
that here we have

00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:44.210
these two terms, which
comprehend these delta xf

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:45.650
and delta tf.

00:20:45.650 --> 00:20:52.330
So we have the variations
in xf and the final time.

00:20:52.330 --> 00:20:56.570
The trick here will be
that we will have to make

00:20:56.570 --> 00:21:00.670
the appropriate "substitutions"
for these delta x terms.

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:03.530
And so very
practically, if we are

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:06.090
in a setting where
delta x-- again,

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:09.330
delta x here represents
the admissible variations

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:13.170
that we can allow at
the final state xf.

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:17.770
So if delta x is fixed, it
means that the only admissible

00:21:17.770 --> 00:21:19.930
variation here is 0.

00:21:19.930 --> 00:21:22.970
And so this entire
term will go to 0.

00:21:22.970 --> 00:21:27.240
And so we're only left with
this other part of the boundary

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:28.280
condition.

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:30.680
Same thing for delta tf.

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:34.360
So we'll be using the fact
that either one or the other

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:39.020
are fixed or free in
order to basically compute

00:21:39.020 --> 00:21:40.520
the additional
boundary conditions

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:42.060
from this equation over here.

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:48.580
And you can imagine there are
a number of different cases.

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:51.080
We won't go through
all of them, but there

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:53.800
are some nice tables
which basically summarize

00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:58.480
all of the cases that we can
find and different combinations.

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:01.280
So, for example, if we
look at the very first one,

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:03.500
this is the setting
we were on Monday.

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:06.720
And in the example that
we just showed where

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:09.300
both xf and tf were specified.

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:11.800
And so this essentially entails
that the boundary conditions

00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:17.520
are the initial and final
state in and of itself.

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:21.840
On the other hand, if
the final x of tf is free

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:29.870
and the final time is specified,
what we get is essentially this,

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:33.110
that the derivative of g with
respect to x dot evaluated

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:36.390
the final time needs
to be equal to 0, which

00:22:36.390 --> 00:22:38.950
is indeed only this part here.

00:22:38.950 --> 00:22:44.330
So if tf is fixed,
delta tf is 0.

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:46.550
And so all of this
gets canceled out.

00:22:46.550 --> 00:22:49.470
And so what remains is
this part over here.

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:51.990
And so we know that
if this is free

00:22:51.990 --> 00:22:55.950
so we can have any possible--
any admissible variation

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.170
in order for all of
this to be equal to 0,

00:22:58.170 --> 00:23:00.010
then this part has
to be equal to 0.

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:05.437
So nothing too crazy here.

00:23:05.437 --> 00:23:07.270
But at the very high
level, what we're doing

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:10.350
is that we are extending
the current formalism.

00:23:10.350 --> 00:23:13.130
When we extend it to
generalized boundary conditions,

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:16.790
we are lacking some
boundary conditions

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:19.830
to pinpoint constants
of integrations.

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:23.650
And we can derive them through
this equation over here,

00:23:23.650 --> 00:23:26.580
which ends up being a
case by case analysis

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:33.260
on essentially pinpointing
those constants of integration.

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:34.280
Does this make sense?

00:23:38.140 --> 00:23:41.340
OK, great.

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:46.060
So now, again, to see this
in practice how this works,

00:23:46.060 --> 00:23:52.220
let's take a very
related problem in a way.

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:54.200
There's [INAUDIBLE]
a boundary condition.

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:57.440
I noticed that the initial
time is not in there.

00:23:57.440 --> 00:24:02.040
Is that you're always
assuming that to be set--

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:02.540
Sorry.

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:03.900
Can you repeat?

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:07.400
I noticed that the initial
time for the x is not in there.

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:11.660
It's only whether or
not xf or tf are set.

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:15.288
Is that because we're-- and
also gets shown in the equation.

00:24:15.288 --> 00:24:16.830
But it's always set
as being initial.

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:18.860
So do we just assume
it's always [INAUDIBLE]?

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:20.842
Yeah, I mean, usually
if you think about it

00:24:20.842 --> 00:24:23.300
from an optimal control problem,
you know where you are now

00:24:23.300 --> 00:24:25.660
and you know what time is now.

00:24:25.660 --> 00:24:28.180
Yeah, but there could be
also some problems where

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:31.342
it's like, when are you going
to start necessarily majoring

00:24:31.342 --> 00:24:32.800
or something like
that perspective,

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:34.660
or it can start any
time you're thinking

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:38.740
of maybe not from a point of
view, maybe from a optimizing

00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:39.847
some type of point.

00:24:39.847 --> 00:24:42.180
And when you're going to make
this decision or something

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:46.260
like that, but then it wouldn't
be able to use this [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:48.660
I think you can always
reformulate your problem such

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:51.360
that you're starting from
0, time 0 and state 0.

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:54.500
You can always reshift
and reorient your problem

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:56.600
to always have the initial
conditions are given.

00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:58.200
Does that make sense?

00:24:58.200 --> 00:24:58.700
Yeah.

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:06.300
And so the example we'll
be looking at specifically

00:25:06.300 --> 00:25:09.000
is one where we
want to determine,

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:11.740
again, the smooth
curve or smaller length

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:31.750
that connects point x0 equals
1 to a free final point x of f,

00:25:31.750 --> 00:25:34.170
but on the line t equals 5.

00:25:34.170 --> 00:25:37.570
So here we're assuming we have a
fixed final time of t equals 5.

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:40.550
We have a fixed initial
condition x of 0 equals 1.

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:45.090
But then we want to find
the curve of shortest length

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:47.030
that connects to this line here.

00:25:47.030 --> 00:25:49.850
So we want to find
the final x of tf.

00:25:49.850 --> 00:25:53.130
So it could be this
line over here.

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:54.560
It could be this line over here.

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:56.310
It could be this other
function over here.

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:59.010
But most likely it
will be this one here.

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:02.850
And so let us now approach
it from the perspective

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:06.970
of the optimality conditions
which we just introduced.

00:26:06.970 --> 00:26:11.490
So again, as we were
doing here above,

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:14.190
the cost function
will be the same.

00:26:14.190 --> 00:26:16.230
So we keep this cost
function over here.

00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:20.940
And we already applied Euler.

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:27.200
So we know from the treatment we
were doing here that the Euler

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:29.080
condition still holds.

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:32.220
So we can leverage the results
that we had here above,

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:35.800
meaning that the Euler
condition essentially boils down

00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:40.852
to this equation over here.

00:26:40.852 --> 00:26:43.660
Which just for practicality
let me rewrite.

00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:50.120
So we derived above that x
of t is equal to c1t plus c2.

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:52.800
Now in this case, we have
one boundary condition, which

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:56.120
is that x of 0 is equal to 1.

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:59.440
And so from these
two we can evaluate

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:07.400
x at time 0, which is equal
to c1 times 0 plus c2.

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:12.160
And this must be equal
to 1, which essentially

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:20.750
means that c2 is equal to 1.

00:27:20.750 --> 00:27:24.750
So we use the initial condition
to pinpoint one of the two

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:27.950
constants of integration.

00:27:27.950 --> 00:27:30.250
Now how do we find
the other one?

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:31.682
So c1.

00:27:31.682 --> 00:27:33.390
We are missing, as we
were saying before,

00:27:33.390 --> 00:27:35.170
one of these
boundary conditions.

00:27:35.170 --> 00:27:39.030
And so we'll use the one we
just arrived on the slide.

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:41.930
And again, we want
to be methodical.

00:27:41.930 --> 00:27:47.270
So the way we do this
is that from the table

00:27:47.270 --> 00:27:51.030
we are in the case
where tf is specified.

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:52.790
But x of tf is free.

00:27:52.790 --> 00:27:55.370
So we already used this
boundary condition over here.

00:27:55.370 --> 00:28:05.790
Now we want to use the second
one, which in practice boils

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:09.670
down to evaluating
the derivative of g

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:14.810
with respect to x dot,
which we did again before.

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:20.860
But let me just rewrite it here,
which is 1 over 2 multiplied

00:28:20.860 --> 00:28:37.100
square root of x1 plus x squared
x dot squared times 2x dot.

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:40.180
And so what we want
in practice is that we

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:41.480
want to take this expression.

00:28:41.480 --> 00:28:43.022
So looking at the
boundary condition,

00:28:43.022 --> 00:28:44.360
we take this expression.

00:28:44.360 --> 00:28:49.280
We evaluate it at time tf,
and we set it equal to 0.

00:28:51.900 --> 00:29:02.340
So this leads to x dot of
tf over square root of 1

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:12.420
plus x dot of tf equal to
0, which essentially means

00:29:12.420 --> 00:29:17.470
that x dot of tf is equal to 0.

00:29:22.575 --> 00:29:29.410
So now we know from
here, we know that x of t

00:29:29.410 --> 00:29:33.570
is equal to c1t
plus 1 because c2

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:50.120
is equal to 1, which essentially
means that x dot of t from here

00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:56.450
is equal to c1 by
differentiating this.

00:29:56.450 --> 00:29:58.570
And so basically,
now what we have

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:01.770
is that by combining these
two we can essentially

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:05.610
derive that c1 is equal to
0 because we are evaluating

00:30:05.610 --> 00:30:06.710
this at the final time.

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:08.490
And we know that
this is equal to 0.

00:30:08.490 --> 00:30:10.430
So c1 must be equal to 0.

00:30:15.156 --> 00:30:19.240
So now what we get
as a final result

00:30:19.240 --> 00:30:23.120
is that we pinpointed both of
our constants of integration.

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:27.340
And so we have that c1 is
equal to 0, c2 is equal to 1.

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:34.120
And so we get an x of
t is equal 1, which

00:30:34.120 --> 00:30:36.260
is indeed this sign right here.

00:30:40.040 --> 00:30:43.600
Again nothing too deep here--
is exactly the same mechanics.

00:30:43.600 --> 00:30:45.960
I've just showed you
an example of how

00:30:45.960 --> 00:30:47.705
do we derive those
additional boundary

00:30:47.705 --> 00:30:49.080
conditions in the
moment we don't

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:52.820
have the necessary boundary
conditions that are needed.

00:31:01.740 --> 00:31:05.360
So in general, just as
a caveat, the moment

00:31:05.360 --> 00:31:09.960
we have a multi-dimensional
functions that we're

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:13.850
dealing with, the exact
same mechanics apply.

00:31:13.850 --> 00:31:16.550
We just apply them
component wise.

00:31:16.550 --> 00:31:18.570
So again, nothing
really deep here.

00:31:18.570 --> 00:31:21.350
We used everything
assuming we had functions

00:31:21.350 --> 00:31:23.450
that mapped reals to reals.

00:31:23.450 --> 00:31:26.730
But in the moment where we have
multi-dimensional functions,

00:31:26.730 --> 00:31:28.870
we would apply all of
the boundary conditions

00:31:28.870 --> 00:31:32.010
and all the Euler
equations per component.

00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:41.670
The second extension that I
want to briefly touch upon--

00:31:41.670 --> 00:31:44.070
I say briefly, because
we will be expanding

00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:46.510
on it in the context
of optimal control

00:31:46.510 --> 00:31:48.110
in a few slides from now--

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:52.270
is the one where we are
considering constraints that

00:31:52.270 --> 00:31:56.652
link together the
function, wt in this case,

00:31:56.652 --> 00:31:59.110
with the derivative of the--
the derivative of the function

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:01.010
wt with the function itself.

00:32:01.010 --> 00:32:01.510
Yes.

00:32:01.510 --> 00:32:05.410
Just on that example, I might
not be seeing something,

00:32:05.410 --> 00:32:09.150
but I'm wondering how you got x
of t equal to 1 and not x of t

00:32:09.150 --> 00:32:12.896
equal to a constant.

00:32:12.896 --> 00:32:17.860
Because we pinpointed this
constant of integration.

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:18.520
This one here.

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:19.020
Where is it?

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:20.360
Yeah, c2 equals 1.

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:20.860
OK.

00:32:20.860 --> 00:32:25.500
So we know that x
of t is c1t plus c2.

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:29.300
And we pinpointed both
c2 is 1 and c1 is 0.

00:32:29.300 --> 00:32:31.400
And so you get x
of t is equal 1.

00:32:40.968 --> 00:32:42.500
So again, what
we're doing here is

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:45.700
we want to bring forward a
second extension that we're

00:32:45.700 --> 00:32:47.560
doing to the current formalism.

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:50.700
Specifically we're interested
in having extensions

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:53.860
to the fact where we have
constraints that tie together

00:32:53.860 --> 00:32:56.740
the derivative of function
with the function itself.

00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:58.740
And so here we're
considering a situation

00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:07.612
where say we have a function w
that maps reals into Rn plus m,

00:33:07.612 --> 00:33:14.500
where we want to essentially
find the extrema for w star

00:33:14.500 --> 00:33:18.300
that basically optimizes this
function over here, which

00:33:18.300 --> 00:33:22.680
is the exact same situation
we were seeing before.

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:26.820
But now we have everything
expressed in this w.

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:31.620
Now notice that the constraints
here are only with respect

00:33:31.620 --> 00:33:34.200
to the first n dimensions.

00:33:34.200 --> 00:33:37.180
So w is a function
that maps in n plus m.

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:39.100
We have n constraints.

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:43.060
And so if you wish a
way you can interpret it

00:33:43.060 --> 00:33:45.620
as taking a step into the
direction of what we care

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:49.560
about is that n could
be our state dimensions,

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:51.340
m could be our controls.

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:54.240
And so we have a
constraint here,

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:57.540
which represents the analog
of the system dynamics, which

00:33:57.540 --> 00:34:00.420
essentially ties together
the controls, which

00:34:00.420 --> 00:34:05.820
are the m free variables
with the n states.

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:09.090
Just a clarification, so
in this situation where

00:34:09.090 --> 00:34:12.610
[INAUDIBLE] assuming
x is a number,

00:34:12.610 --> 00:34:15.610
now you're calling it
w, making it a vector,

00:34:15.610 --> 00:34:18.210
and then you're also
adding [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:21.889
Yes, exactly.

00:34:21.889 --> 00:34:26.409
So if you wish the extension
of calling it w and being it

00:34:26.409 --> 00:34:29.850
a vector valued function is
just extending from function

00:34:29.850 --> 00:34:32.108
to map real to reals into
vector valued functions.

00:34:32.108 --> 00:34:33.650
The only thing that
we're adding here

00:34:33.650 --> 00:34:35.350
is this type of constraints.

00:34:35.350 --> 00:34:35.850
Yeah.

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:43.570
And so indeed we
mentioned that only m

00:34:43.570 --> 00:34:47.370
of the n plus n components
of w are independent.

00:34:47.370 --> 00:34:51.530
And constraints of this type may
represent the state equations,

00:34:51.530 --> 00:34:53.449
such as the system dynamics.

00:34:53.449 --> 00:34:56.770
And so indeed maybe one way
to interpret it is just w

00:34:56.770 --> 00:34:58.530
is the concatenation
of our states

00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:02.250
and controls variables--
control functions.

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:07.720
And so the way we handle this is
similar to the way in which we

00:35:07.720 --> 00:35:11.360
handled in finite dimensional
optimization, the constrained

00:35:11.360 --> 00:35:12.060
setting.

00:35:12.060 --> 00:35:14.580
And specifically in finite
dimensional optimization,

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:18.260
if you remember, we introduced
the concept of Lagrangian,

00:35:18.260 --> 00:35:21.140
where essentially, we
took the constraint.

00:35:21.140 --> 00:35:23.960
We added it to the
objective function

00:35:23.960 --> 00:35:25.880
together with some
Lagrange multipliers,

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:28.120
which we called lambda.

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:31.920
And then we treated the problem
as if it was unconstrained.

00:35:31.920 --> 00:35:34.080
So essentially this term
represented a penalty

00:35:34.080 --> 00:35:36.880
for violating those constraints.

00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:38.720
And so here what
we do is exactly

00:35:38.720 --> 00:35:41.320
the same thing in
the sense that we

00:35:41.320 --> 00:35:45.320
posit an augmented
integrand function g.

00:35:45.320 --> 00:35:52.440
Specifically g of a, which is
the original g plus this term

00:35:52.440 --> 00:35:55.400
here, which represents
the constraint multiplied

00:35:55.400 --> 00:35:59.640
by this p of t, which is
the analog of the Lagrangian

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:00.840
multipliers.

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:03.820
Now notice that in the finite
dimensional optimization,

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:06.270
lambdas were just
a vector of values.

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:10.990
Now, p of t is a function
of time in and of itself,

00:36:10.990 --> 00:36:14.270
and is usually referred to
as the costate in literature.

00:36:14.270 --> 00:36:19.550
Costate because you have
one function for every state

00:36:19.550 --> 00:36:23.150
that you have in your system.

00:36:23.150 --> 00:36:24.950
Yeah, again, so we're
changing the notation

00:36:24.950 --> 00:36:28.150
because this is what you
will find in literature most

00:36:28.150 --> 00:36:28.810
commonly.

00:36:28.810 --> 00:36:32.190
But this is exactly the
analog of the lambda variables

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:34.290
that we introduced
in finite dimensions.

00:36:39.350 --> 00:36:43.790
So the point here is
that we are extending

00:36:43.790 --> 00:36:45.790
our necessary
optimality conditions

00:36:45.790 --> 00:36:47.810
to various extensions.

00:36:47.810 --> 00:36:51.830
And so what you get is that
the optimality conditions

00:36:51.830 --> 00:36:52.850
for this case.

00:36:52.850 --> 00:36:55.670
So the case where we are
introducing this constraints

00:36:55.670 --> 00:37:00.390
essentially boils down to
enforcing the Euler equation--

00:37:00.390 --> 00:37:02.950
this time on the augmented cost.

00:37:02.950 --> 00:37:04.700
So again, we're using
the exact same trick

00:37:04.700 --> 00:37:05.960
we did in finite dimensions.

00:37:05.960 --> 00:37:07.217
So we took the constraint.

00:37:07.217 --> 00:37:08.800
We added it to the
objective function.

00:37:08.800 --> 00:37:11.660
And we're treating it as
if it was unconstrained.

00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:14.160
And so this is exactly the same
thing that we're doing here.

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:16.380
We're taking our
differential constraints,

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:18.980
adding them to the
original cost function g,

00:37:18.980 --> 00:37:20.940
defining the
augmented cost g of a.

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:27.580
And applying the Euler
equation to this g of a.

00:37:27.580 --> 00:37:30.100
Of course, here there is
an additional condition

00:37:30.100 --> 00:37:33.980
that is the fact that the
constraints must be satisfied.

00:37:33.980 --> 00:37:36.380
So if a function
is optimal, it has

00:37:36.380 --> 00:37:38.880
to be also satisfying
those constraints.

00:37:45.180 --> 00:37:48.300
And indeed notice, again,
the similarity with respect

00:37:48.300 --> 00:37:52.180
to the finite
dimensional optimization.

00:37:52.180 --> 00:37:55.518
Again, this I just wanted
to share the intuition

00:37:55.518 --> 00:37:56.560
of how we deal with this.

00:37:56.560 --> 00:37:58.800
We'll formalize it in
a couple of slides.

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:04.950
And so given these
two extensions,

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:07.850
we were treating them from the
broader perspective of calculus

00:38:07.850 --> 00:38:11.130
of variation, which is this
tool, mathematical tool

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:14.590
we're using to solve
problems over functions.

00:38:14.590 --> 00:38:17.130
But now we want to go into
things that we actually

00:38:17.130 --> 00:38:19.830
care about, which is solving
optimal control problems.

00:38:19.830 --> 00:38:23.810
And we will do this in
three steps, actually, four.

00:38:23.810 --> 00:38:26.210
Today we'll only look
at the first one, which

00:38:26.210 --> 00:38:29.610
is we will be deriving
necessary optimality

00:38:29.610 --> 00:38:32.670
conditions for optimal
control problems,

00:38:32.670 --> 00:38:37.270
assuming that the admissible
controls are not bounded.

00:38:37.270 --> 00:38:41.270
So we won't be
assuming constraints,

00:38:41.270 --> 00:38:45.730
such as maximum thrust
on the controls.

00:38:45.730 --> 00:38:48.890
On Monday next week,
we will be introducing

00:38:48.890 --> 00:38:53.010
the celebrated Pontryagin's
Minimum Principle, which

00:38:53.010 --> 00:38:54.570
is essentially
the generalization

00:38:54.570 --> 00:38:57.330
of these optimality
conditions to the case

00:38:57.330 --> 00:39:01.920
where the controls
may be bounded.

00:39:01.920 --> 00:39:04.880
And then we will apply
these optimality conditions

00:39:04.880 --> 00:39:07.560
to a number of
known cases, showing

00:39:07.560 --> 00:39:11.060
that we can derive known
strategies for control,

00:39:11.060 --> 00:39:13.000
such as bang-bang
controls, to those of you

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:14.360
that are familiar with that.

00:39:14.360 --> 00:39:16.280
And then the fourth
that is not listed here

00:39:16.280 --> 00:39:19.480
is that we will be looking
at computational methods

00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:22.160
for solving these
differential equations.

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:26.320
So until now, we've solved
these problems analytically

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:27.680
if you wish.

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:31.880
In practice how you will
use them in this class

00:39:31.880 --> 00:39:33.680
and potentially
in your career is

00:39:33.680 --> 00:39:36.600
that you will be using the
optimality conditions to derive

00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:38.200
these differential equations.

00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:40.040
You derive the
boundary conditions

00:39:40.040 --> 00:39:41.920
for your specific
case, and then you

00:39:41.920 --> 00:39:44.720
feed both so the differential
equations and the boundary

00:39:44.720 --> 00:39:47.560
conditions to a numerical
solver, which will take

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:49.840
care of the solution for you.

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:52.840
And so this is why indirect
methods are also called

00:39:52.840 --> 00:39:54.500
optimized and discretized.

00:39:54.500 --> 00:39:57.580
Because here we're formulating
the optimal solution.

00:39:57.580 --> 00:40:00.350
So the optimized part and
then the discretization part

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:03.430
usually comes when you feed
this into a numerical solver.

00:40:06.130 --> 00:40:08.950
Does the plan make sense?

00:40:08.950 --> 00:40:10.330
And any questions so far?

00:40:18.230 --> 00:40:24.150
So let us consider now the
optimal control problem

00:40:24.150 --> 00:40:26.110
with unbounded controls.

00:40:26.110 --> 00:40:30.790
So specifically, as we
introduced also a couple times

00:40:30.790 --> 00:40:32.830
in the previous
lectures, the problem

00:40:32.830 --> 00:40:36.630
here is we want to find an
admissible control u star, which

00:40:36.630 --> 00:40:40.310
causes the dynamical system
represented by this differential

00:40:40.310 --> 00:40:44.190
equation to follow an
admissible trajectory x of star

00:40:44.190 --> 00:40:48.230
that minimizes the
following cost functional.

00:40:48.230 --> 00:40:53.070
The cost functional is
composed of two terms--

00:40:53.070 --> 00:40:56.940
stage cost represented
by g, which is

00:40:56.940 --> 00:40:59.860
a function of x and u and time.

00:40:59.860 --> 00:41:04.300
Usually, g is something that
is accumulated over time

00:41:04.300 --> 00:41:07.780
and can represent things
like control effort.

00:41:07.780 --> 00:41:10.180
So maybe something
like u squared.

00:41:10.180 --> 00:41:13.660
So you don't want to maybe
push too much on the throttle

00:41:13.660 --> 00:41:15.500
or something like that.

00:41:15.500 --> 00:41:21.220
And terminal cost, which
depends only on the final time.

00:41:21.220 --> 00:41:22.683
So the state of the final time.

00:41:22.683 --> 00:41:24.100
And this could
represent something

00:41:24.100 --> 00:41:26.620
like making sure that you're
actually hitting the target

00:41:26.620 --> 00:41:27.640
that you want to hit.

00:41:30.580 --> 00:41:35.980
Notice that j-- so the cost
is only a function of u.

00:41:35.980 --> 00:41:40.220
Because this [? u ?] are our
free variables, our controls,

00:41:40.220 --> 00:41:43.820
and the state that correspond
to our control signal

00:41:43.820 --> 00:41:48.580
are implicitly defined
through the system dynamics.

00:41:48.580 --> 00:41:51.220
So this is the setup
we introduced before.

00:41:51.220 --> 00:41:52.940
But just making
sure that this is

00:41:52.940 --> 00:41:56.890
exactly where we're going into.

00:41:56.890 --> 00:42:00.490
And we are assuming
that the initial time

00:42:00.490 --> 00:42:02.430
and initial
conditions are fixed.

00:42:06.530 --> 00:42:11.770
So the way I will go by deriving
the optimality conditions

00:42:11.770 --> 00:42:13.230
is in three steps.

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:15.490
So first I'll state them.

00:42:15.490 --> 00:42:17.650
Then we will go
through an example

00:42:17.650 --> 00:42:19.170
showing how to apply them.

00:42:19.170 --> 00:42:21.450
And then we'll go through
a sketch of a proof of how

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:24.490
we can derive these results.

00:42:24.490 --> 00:42:29.550
So the optimality conditions are
nothing more than this slide,

00:42:29.550 --> 00:42:32.090
nothing less than
this slide over here.

00:42:32.090 --> 00:42:35.770
So specifically, the
optimality conditions

00:42:35.770 --> 00:42:40.850
state that we define the
Hamiltonian meaning we augment

00:42:40.850 --> 00:42:43.230
the cost with the constraint.

00:42:43.230 --> 00:42:46.810
So exactly how we did before
through the equivalent

00:42:46.810 --> 00:42:51.740
of the Lagrangian or
costate variables p of t.

00:42:51.740 --> 00:42:55.930
And then define the
optimality conditions

00:42:55.930 --> 00:43:02.650
whereby an optimal x, p,
u triplet must satisfy

00:43:02.650 --> 00:43:04.350
some differential equations.

00:43:04.350 --> 00:43:07.450
We'll have a look
at them more later.

00:43:07.450 --> 00:43:09.650
Given some boundary
conditions, which

00:43:09.650 --> 00:43:12.030
are given through this
equation over here,

00:43:12.030 --> 00:43:14.370
which we can handle
exactly how we did before,

00:43:14.370 --> 00:43:21.147
depending on if our final
time or xf are fixed or not.

00:43:21.147 --> 00:43:22.730
So let's have a look
a little bit more

00:43:22.730 --> 00:43:26.470
closely at what these
differential equations look

00:43:26.470 --> 00:43:26.970
like.

00:43:26.970 --> 00:43:30.890
So the first one is obvious
because these are essentially

00:43:30.890 --> 00:43:33.290
the dynamics constraints.

00:43:33.290 --> 00:43:36.610
So if you look at
the Hamiltonian

00:43:36.610 --> 00:43:40.530
here and take the
derivative with respect

00:43:40.530 --> 00:43:44.330
to the costate p of t,
what you're left with

00:43:44.330 --> 00:43:47.750
is that you're left
with the dynamics.

00:43:47.750 --> 00:43:49.510
You're left with f.

00:43:49.510 --> 00:43:51.690
So essentially this
condition over here

00:43:51.690 --> 00:43:54.860
is just saying that for
a triplet to be optimal,

00:43:54.860 --> 00:43:57.080
it needs to satisfy
the dynamics.

00:43:57.080 --> 00:43:58.360
Make sense.

00:43:58.360 --> 00:44:00.940
The second and third
are less intuitive.

00:44:00.940 --> 00:44:03.160
We'll derive them in a bit.

00:44:03.160 --> 00:44:05.480
But very mechanically,
what you typically do

00:44:05.480 --> 00:44:10.440
is that you would essentially
define the Hamiltonian.

00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:13.840
You then posit these
differential equations.

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:18.340
You use the third which are m
algebraic equations-- actually,

00:44:18.340 --> 00:44:20.120
this is not a
differential equation--

00:44:20.120 --> 00:44:24.960
to express u as a
function of p and x.

00:44:24.960 --> 00:44:28.600
And then you basically solve
these differential equations

00:44:28.600 --> 00:44:31.320
using the boundary
conditions to pinpoint

00:44:31.320 --> 00:44:32.900
the constants of integration.

00:44:36.280 --> 00:44:36.800
Yes.

00:44:36.800 --> 00:44:38.665
What is [INAUDIBLE]?

00:44:38.665 --> 00:44:40.420
Yes, so it's a good question.

00:44:40.420 --> 00:44:42.140
It's important
that this is clear.

00:44:42.140 --> 00:44:46.080
So remember how we were looking
before at the case where

00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:50.670
we introduced the constraints
represented by the dynamics.

00:44:50.670 --> 00:44:54.510
And in order to handle these,
we used a very similar logic

00:44:54.510 --> 00:44:57.830
that from the one that we used
in finite dimensions, which

00:44:57.830 --> 00:45:00.730
is when we have a constraint
in order to handle it,

00:45:00.730 --> 00:45:03.830
we take that constraint
and we augment the cost

00:45:03.830 --> 00:45:06.130
with the penalty
for that constraint.

00:45:06.130 --> 00:45:09.270
And then we treat the problem
as if it was unconstrained.

00:45:09.270 --> 00:45:11.550
And so in finite
dimensions, we did this

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:14.190
through Lagrange
multipliers, which

00:45:14.190 --> 00:45:16.950
are the lambdas if you recall.

00:45:16.950 --> 00:45:20.290
And the costate here is
playing the exact same role,

00:45:20.290 --> 00:45:25.670
but this time in the context
of continuous time functions.

00:45:25.670 --> 00:45:29.710
And it represents the analog of
the Lagrange multipliers, which

00:45:29.710 --> 00:45:33.670
we call costate and
label them as p.

00:45:33.670 --> 00:45:34.610
Does that make sense?

00:45:44.070 --> 00:45:46.230
Any other question on this?

00:45:46.230 --> 00:45:51.060
So again, here I'm just stating
what the optimality conditions

00:45:51.060 --> 00:45:54.300
are for the optimal control
problem we're interested in.

00:45:54.300 --> 00:45:57.020
And these optimality
conditions look

00:45:57.020 --> 00:46:02.460
as this set of
differential equations

00:46:02.460 --> 00:46:05.860
with accompanying
boundary conditions.

00:46:05.860 --> 00:46:08.920
And mechanically, we
will see how to use them.

00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:12.380
But it is mechanically
it's a process where

00:46:12.380 --> 00:46:17.220
we compose the Hamiltonian
by essentially augmenting

00:46:17.220 --> 00:46:19.740
the cost through our dynamics.

00:46:19.740 --> 00:46:22.380
We define these
differential equations

00:46:22.380 --> 00:46:25.440
and then we solve for them by
using the boundary conditions.

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:34.500
As we were doing before,
of course, in order

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:39.540
to find the correct
boundary conditions,

00:46:39.540 --> 00:46:41.880
it's also a case
by case scenario.

00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:45.220
And so we have equivalent
tables as we're

00:46:45.220 --> 00:46:47.490
having in the previous case.

00:46:47.490 --> 00:46:48.770
Yes.

00:46:48.770 --> 00:46:50.940
I still have a question
about the previous slide.

00:46:50.940 --> 00:46:51.690
Could you restate?

00:46:51.690 --> 00:46:56.210
You were saying something
you [INAUDIBLE] for u

00:46:56.210 --> 00:46:58.330
and then do something with that?

00:46:58.330 --> 00:46:59.890
Yeah, so we will
see it in a moment

00:46:59.890 --> 00:47:01.370
because we will do an example.

00:47:01.370 --> 00:47:03.810
But what I meant is
that-- so in practice

00:47:03.810 --> 00:47:07.430
after you define the
Hamiltonian, applying these.

00:47:07.430 --> 00:47:08.910
So using them to
solve a problem,

00:47:08.910 --> 00:47:11.330
it means that you need
to basically solve

00:47:11.330 --> 00:47:13.090
these differential equations.

00:47:13.090 --> 00:47:15.490
And in practice,
what usually happens

00:47:15.490 --> 00:47:18.290
is that you use the
third one to express u

00:47:18.290 --> 00:47:19.770
as a function of p and x.

00:47:19.770 --> 00:47:22.050
And then you use these two.

00:47:22.050 --> 00:47:27.410
Basically, substitute u in
the first two differential

00:47:27.410 --> 00:47:28.230
equations.

00:47:28.230 --> 00:47:30.410
And then you solve these
differential equations

00:47:30.410 --> 00:47:32.570
pinpointing the
constants of integration

00:47:32.570 --> 00:47:35.650
with the boundary conditions.

00:47:35.650 --> 00:47:36.650
Yes.

00:47:36.650 --> 00:47:39.810
Just trying to connect what we
were looking at before, this

00:47:39.810 --> 00:47:40.370
here.

00:47:40.370 --> 00:47:42.970
So it's we're using the same
boundary conditions that we just

00:47:42.970 --> 00:47:44.010
talked about.

00:47:44.010 --> 00:47:47.500
It's just to create a case of
not using these-- that you use,

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:49.280
as well, right?

00:47:49.280 --> 00:47:52.200
I mean, the boundary conditions
look slightly different as well.

00:47:52.200 --> 00:47:56.360
So this is where I am
just stating the result.

00:47:56.360 --> 00:47:59.900
I'm happy to provide the
derivations of how we get there.

00:47:59.900 --> 00:48:01.760
We'll have a sketch
of a proof, so we'll

00:48:01.760 --> 00:48:03.320
be some intuition there.

00:48:03.320 --> 00:48:05.360
But the point is that
the derivations are not

00:48:05.360 --> 00:48:06.920
that insightful.

00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:07.738
Yeah.

00:48:07.738 --> 00:48:10.280
But yes, the point is that you
have some boundary conditions,

00:48:10.280 --> 00:48:12.572
which are not exactly the
same as we were doing before.

00:48:12.572 --> 00:48:15.412
Mechanically, we use them
exactly in the same way,

00:48:15.412 --> 00:48:16.620
but they just look different.

00:48:16.620 --> 00:48:17.240
A little bit different.

00:48:17.240 --> 00:48:17.740
Yeah.

00:48:17.740 --> 00:48:18.377
Thanks.

00:48:18.377 --> 00:48:20.960
The difference between what we
were previously doing before it

00:48:20.960 --> 00:48:21.940
was a lot of notations.

00:48:21.940 --> 00:48:23.280
It just changed.

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:25.000
The main difference
is more instead

00:48:25.000 --> 00:48:28.660
of just finding a position
or [INAUDIBLE] x of t,

00:48:28.660 --> 00:48:32.360
we're now doing it as a
control system of equations?

00:48:32.360 --> 00:48:34.640
Yeah, so I can
understand the confusion

00:48:34.640 --> 00:48:36.660
that comes with a lot
of notation changing.

00:48:36.660 --> 00:48:39.440
So the point is
that up until now

00:48:39.440 --> 00:48:41.560
we introduced
calculus of variation

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:44.830
as a tool for solving infinite
dimensional optimization

00:48:44.830 --> 00:48:47.570
problems detached
from optimal control.

00:48:47.570 --> 00:48:53.270
And so we are using only x or w
as a combination of everything.

00:48:53.270 --> 00:48:57.950
Now we are basically doing
the hop in the direction

00:48:57.950 --> 00:48:59.290
of optimal control.

00:48:59.290 --> 00:49:03.870
And we want to use the exact
same tools for now formulating

00:49:03.870 --> 00:49:07.590
everything notation included
as an optimal control problem

00:49:07.590 --> 00:49:10.710
where we have the dynamics
f, we have the states x,

00:49:10.710 --> 00:49:13.230
and we have the controls u.

00:49:13.230 --> 00:49:15.310
Does it clarify?

00:49:15.310 --> 00:49:19.950
Yes, and so the equations
are slightly changing to

00:49:19.950 --> 00:49:21.710
[INAUDIBLE].

00:49:21.710 --> 00:49:24.028
But the equation it
looks extremely similar.

00:49:24.028 --> 00:49:26.070
And that's on purpose
because the only difference

00:49:26.070 --> 00:49:27.190
is we're adding the u.

00:49:27.190 --> 00:49:30.030
It's like [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:30.030 --> 00:49:32.850
I mean, what equation
are you referring to?

00:49:32.850 --> 00:49:34.590
This one here.

00:49:34.590 --> 00:49:36.790
Yeah, that equation
there, essentially, u just

00:49:36.790 --> 00:49:38.710
became x dot, well,
it was previously

00:49:38.710 --> 00:49:40.450
x dot in the previous time.

00:49:40.450 --> 00:49:42.670
And now it's u because
we are now controlling

00:49:42.670 --> 00:49:44.660
that versus [INAUDIBLE].

00:49:44.660 --> 00:49:47.860
If now we are
controlling the system

00:49:47.860 --> 00:49:50.380
through the coupling
of-- so u free variables,

00:49:50.380 --> 00:49:53.220
we control u's. And we
control x through u.

00:49:53.220 --> 00:49:56.420
Before we could
control directly x.

00:49:56.420 --> 00:49:57.220
Yeah.

00:49:57.220 --> 00:49:58.740
Yeah.

00:49:58.740 --> 00:49:59.520
Yes.

00:49:59.520 --> 00:50:00.020
Sorry.

00:50:00.020 --> 00:50:01.980
I'm just confused with
what's the difference

00:50:01.980 --> 00:50:09.540
between the Hamiltonian and the
ga function defined earlier.

00:50:09.540 --> 00:50:16.740
Yes, so the ga function,
you should see it as--

00:50:16.740 --> 00:50:19.900
basically, I wanted to give
you the intuition of how

00:50:19.900 --> 00:50:26.140
to handle this constraints that
couples in the optimal control

00:50:26.140 --> 00:50:28.940
notation couples
controls with states.

00:50:28.940 --> 00:50:30.860
And I did this.

00:50:30.860 --> 00:50:33.760
Here we had a simplified
objective function.

00:50:33.760 --> 00:50:36.180
We only had this g over here.

00:50:36.180 --> 00:50:39.680
And so in this specific
case augmenting.

00:50:39.680 --> 00:50:42.850
So taking these constraints
into the objective function

00:50:42.850 --> 00:50:46.290
and penalizing for them boiled
down to this augmented G.

00:50:46.290 --> 00:50:47.770
In the more general
setting, which

00:50:47.770 --> 00:50:50.410
is the one we care
about, we formally

00:50:50.410 --> 00:50:53.810
define this Hamiltonian,
which is, again, intuitively

00:50:53.810 --> 00:50:58.270
the same thing, but this is a
much more general formalism,

00:50:58.270 --> 00:50:59.456
if that makes sense.

00:50:59.456 --> 00:50:59.956
OK.

00:51:02.536 --> 00:51:05.010
Yes.

00:51:05.010 --> 00:51:07.195
Is the Hamiltonian the
same as the Lagrangian

00:51:07.195 --> 00:51:08.570
we had in previous
lectures where

00:51:08.570 --> 00:51:10.490
we had the lambdas and stuff?

00:51:10.490 --> 00:51:12.450
You could interpret it
as the indeed the analog

00:51:12.450 --> 00:51:13.350
of the Lagrangian.

00:51:13.350 --> 00:51:13.850
Yeah.

00:51:20.790 --> 00:51:25.530
And so in practice, the
necessary conditions,

00:51:25.530 --> 00:51:31.170
meaning these ones here,
essentially represent system

00:51:31.170 --> 00:51:36.210
of 2n differential
equations of-- well,

00:51:36.210 --> 00:51:40.200
a set of second order, 2 n first
order differential equations

00:51:40.200 --> 00:51:44.920
and algebraic equations,
which is the third.

00:51:44.920 --> 00:51:48.440
And in order to solve
these, we would basically

00:51:48.440 --> 00:51:52.340
need to pinpoint these
constants of integrations.

00:51:52.340 --> 00:51:57.040
And so we will do this by
using the boundary conditions

00:51:57.040 --> 00:51:58.480
as we were doing before.

00:51:58.480 --> 00:52:00.647
But this is just to give
you a sense that at the end

00:52:00.647 --> 00:52:04.040
of the day, we'll have 2n
constants of integrations.

00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:10.240
And we will need 2n boundary
conditions to pinpoint those

00:52:10.240 --> 00:52:12.440
constants of integration.

00:52:12.440 --> 00:52:16.840
Actually, so here I'm
mentioning 2n plus 1.

00:52:16.840 --> 00:52:19.860
Can you think of the case
where we need the plus 1.

00:52:22.720 --> 00:52:25.200
So again, here we're just
mechanically thinking of we'll

00:52:25.200 --> 00:52:27.720
have a set of
differential equations.

00:52:27.720 --> 00:52:31.840
And we need the appropriate
number of boundary conditions

00:52:31.840 --> 00:52:34.860
to pinpoint those
constants of integrations.

00:52:40.600 --> 00:52:42.020
And we will see
it in the example,

00:52:42.020 --> 00:52:43.600
but essentially,
the additional one

00:52:43.600 --> 00:52:47.440
will be to pinpoint
the final time.

00:52:47.440 --> 00:52:50.520
But now I think it's good
that we go through an example

00:52:50.520 --> 00:52:51.980
to see this mechanically.

00:53:21.920 --> 00:53:26.480
All right, so the problem
we'll be looking at

00:53:26.480 --> 00:53:32.520
is the one where we are
trying to essentially optimize

00:53:32.520 --> 00:53:36.240
the equivalent of a
particle on a line.

00:53:36.240 --> 00:53:43.750
So we have x of t, which
represents position

00:53:43.750 --> 00:53:46.270
of our particle at time t.

00:53:46.270 --> 00:53:51.230
And this is defined
by a system, which

00:53:51.230 --> 00:53:56.567
is a second order through some
second order dynamics, which

00:53:56.567 --> 00:53:58.150
essentially means
that we are directly

00:53:58.150 --> 00:54:01.270
controlling the acceleration
of this particle.

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:04.350
So we're assuming a unit mass.

00:54:04.350 --> 00:54:06.470
And so F equals ma.

00:54:06.470 --> 00:54:10.750
We are directly controlling the
acceleration of this particle.

00:54:10.750 --> 00:54:15.510
And specifically, we
want to steer this system

00:54:15.510 --> 00:54:23.830
from an initial condition
whereby at time 0, so x of 0

00:54:23.830 --> 00:54:24.850
is equal to 10.

00:54:24.850 --> 00:54:31.590
So we are at a position
10 and with 0 velocity.

00:54:31.590 --> 00:54:38.220
Because the derivative of
x at time 0 is equal to 0.

00:54:38.220 --> 00:54:41.820
And we want to reach the origin.

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:45.940
So we want that x
of tf is equal to 0.

00:54:45.940 --> 00:54:52.380
And also we want to
reach it with 0 velocity.

00:54:52.380 --> 00:54:55.880
So this is what we want
to do in the system.

00:54:55.880 --> 00:54:59.620
And specifically, we want
to do this while minimizing

00:54:59.620 --> 00:55:05.940
the following function j, which
is comprised of two parts.

00:55:05.940 --> 00:55:12.700
We have a running cost, which
is defined as 1/2 the integral

00:55:12.700 --> 00:55:15.670
between t0 and tf of bu--

00:55:15.670 --> 00:55:17.220
[? ut ?] squared.

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:20.580
Now this essentially
represents the analog

00:55:20.580 --> 00:55:24.620
of wanting to minimize
control effort, for example.

00:55:24.620 --> 00:55:29.520
And we have a terminal cost that
is 1/2 times alpha tf squared,

00:55:29.520 --> 00:55:35.050
which represents essentially
wanting to get to the objective

00:55:35.050 --> 00:55:36.230
quickly.

00:55:36.230 --> 00:55:38.510
Essentially, we don't want
to take a very long time,

00:55:38.510 --> 00:55:40.530
so we're penalizing final time.

00:55:40.530 --> 00:55:44.130
Now these two terms are
balanced and weighted

00:55:44.130 --> 00:55:47.730
from these two scaling
factors alpha and beta.

00:55:47.730 --> 00:55:51.250
And so depending on
which one is bigger,

00:55:51.250 --> 00:55:54.370
we might want to reach
our objective by--

00:55:54.370 --> 00:55:56.490
so if b is very large,
we want to do this

00:55:56.490 --> 00:56:02.090
by not be really cautious with
the acceleration that we have.

00:56:02.090 --> 00:56:03.870
On the opposite, if
alpha is very large,

00:56:03.870 --> 00:56:05.670
we want to reach
it very quickly,

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:09.930
potentially at the expense
of larger control effort.

00:56:09.930 --> 00:56:11.050
Yes.

00:56:11.050 --> 00:56:14.270
I just wanted to using that half
part that just says minimized.

00:56:14.270 --> 00:56:17.170
So you don't want as much
[? acceleration ?] as possible.

00:56:17.170 --> 00:56:18.430
Yes, exactly.

00:56:18.430 --> 00:56:19.770
Yeah.

00:56:19.770 --> 00:56:21.645
Yeah, so we are
in a setting where

00:56:21.645 --> 00:56:22.770
we are defining a function.

00:56:22.770 --> 00:56:25.290
And in general, we're
always minimizing

00:56:25.290 --> 00:56:29.090
costs in our optimal
control problems.

00:56:29.090 --> 00:56:33.008
And in this case, the final
time is assumed to be free.

00:56:33.008 --> 00:56:37.680
Are these 1/2 and squared kind
of how they do the cost or is

00:56:37.680 --> 00:56:38.680
that what--

00:56:38.680 --> 00:56:39.700
This is just a choice.

00:56:39.700 --> 00:56:42.340
Yeah.

00:56:42.340 --> 00:56:44.540
So why is this interesting?

00:56:44.540 --> 00:56:46.760
I think it is
particularly interesting

00:56:46.760 --> 00:56:50.680
because it's a simple
example that encapsulates

00:56:50.680 --> 00:56:55.360
a lot of the characteristics of
what you would see in real world

00:56:55.360 --> 00:56:56.660
optimal control problems.

00:56:56.660 --> 00:56:59.920
So we have.

00:56:59.920 --> 00:57:03.500
Some cost, which
balances control effort.

00:57:03.500 --> 00:57:05.800
And the final time, we
have a free final time

00:57:05.800 --> 00:57:08.600
which we want to optimize over.

00:57:08.600 --> 00:57:11.280
And in general, it's
a problem that if I

00:57:11.280 --> 00:57:14.500
were to have given this to
you a couple of weeks back,

00:57:14.500 --> 00:57:16.880
you probably wouldn't have
known how to start with.

00:57:16.880 --> 00:57:20.520
And now we indeed have
a general formalism

00:57:20.520 --> 00:57:23.920
which we could use to tackle
these kind of problems.

00:57:23.920 --> 00:57:28.640
So let's get started.

00:57:28.640 --> 00:57:33.170
So in general, in
optimal control,

00:57:33.170 --> 00:57:35.790
we want to be methodical.

00:57:35.790 --> 00:57:41.008
So let's apply and follow
step by step the recipe.

00:57:41.008 --> 00:57:42.550
So the first thing
that we want to do

00:57:42.550 --> 00:57:47.550
is define the Hamiltonian,
which we defined

00:57:47.550 --> 00:57:52.350
as g plus our costate
variables, again,

00:57:52.350 --> 00:57:59.510
the analog of the Lagrangian,
multipliers transpose f.

00:57:59.510 --> 00:58:02.610
Now, in order to
apply any of this,

00:58:02.610 --> 00:58:06.670
the first thing that
we have to do is--

00:58:06.670 --> 00:58:09.650
so remember that you
might have noticed,

00:58:09.650 --> 00:58:13.470
but here we're looking
at a second order system.

00:58:13.470 --> 00:58:15.470
And everything that
we derived so far

00:58:15.470 --> 00:58:18.210
assumes we have a
first order system.

00:58:18.210 --> 00:58:19.790
So the first thing
that we want to do

00:58:19.790 --> 00:58:23.250
is cast this problem as a first
order differential equation,

00:58:23.250 --> 00:58:25.310
which we know how to do.

00:58:25.310 --> 00:58:27.630
And specifically
the way we do this

00:58:27.630 --> 00:58:33.100
is by constructing the
analog of this system, which

00:58:33.100 --> 00:58:39.860
is essentially by adding an
additional dimension whereby x.1

00:58:39.860 --> 00:58:48.340
is equal to x2 and
x.2 is equal to u.

00:58:48.340 --> 00:58:53.260
So we saw this I think
Monday last week.

00:58:53.260 --> 00:58:57.060
And for us this here will be f.

00:59:00.620 --> 00:59:10.020
Now, remember that we have one
costate variable for every state

00:59:10.020 --> 00:59:11.300
variable.

00:59:11.300 --> 00:59:21.660
And so we define p of t as
the vector of p1t and p2t.

00:59:21.660 --> 00:59:23.520
So we essentially
have two components.

00:59:26.100 --> 00:59:28.530
But so now we have
all of the ingredients

00:59:28.530 --> 00:59:32.570
to define our Hamiltonian.

00:59:32.570 --> 00:59:39.210
So h will be g, which from our--

00:59:39.210 --> 00:59:41.930
actually it's probably useful
to keep the problem over here.

00:59:41.930 --> 00:59:51.410
From our definition of the
functional, g is 1/2 bu squared.

00:59:51.410 --> 00:59:54.250
So again, this is g.

00:59:54.250 --> 01:00:02.490
Plus the vector of costate
variables p1t to p2t.

01:00:02.490 --> 01:00:25.690
Transpose x to t, so our fu,
which we can expand as 1/2 bu

01:00:25.690 --> 01:00:37.860
squared t plus p1t
x2t plus p to t ut.

01:00:41.200 --> 01:00:46.380
I hope this is big enough
for the ones in the back.

01:00:46.380 --> 01:00:46.880
Yeah.

01:00:51.480 --> 01:00:57.600
So now that we have
the Hamiltonian,

01:00:57.600 --> 01:01:00.640
let's be methodical
and indeed define

01:01:00.640 --> 01:01:03.220
our differential equations,
our optimality conditions.

01:01:03.220 --> 01:01:03.720
Yes.

01:01:03.720 --> 01:01:05.200
Do [INAUDIBLE]?

01:01:05.200 --> 01:01:09.360
Let's say it was just your own
controlling velocity instead

01:01:09.360 --> 01:01:13.640
of the acceleration,
what would we

01:01:13.640 --> 01:01:16.860
have [INAUDIBLE] would
just be x itself?

01:01:16.860 --> 01:01:19.080
And then would there
still be the u?

01:01:19.080 --> 01:01:20.380
Or [INAUDIBLE]?

01:01:24.240 --> 01:01:26.970
Now, I'm not exactly sure
about this specific example,

01:01:26.970 --> 01:01:28.720
but in general, if you
have a system where

01:01:28.720 --> 01:01:31.600
it would be just a first order
system, it would be as it is.

01:01:31.600 --> 01:01:33.580
It would be ready to apply it.

01:01:33.580 --> 01:01:34.080
Now--

01:01:34.080 --> 01:01:37.560
Would it be a two-- would it
still be a two-dimensional--

01:01:37.560 --> 01:01:38.700
No, in that case--

01:01:38.700 --> 01:01:40.460
It would be [? p and f ?]
essentially.

01:01:40.460 --> 01:01:40.960
Yes.

01:01:46.160 --> 01:01:52.520
So let us take and define
the first of our conditions,

01:01:52.520 --> 01:01:57.840
which is that x dot has to
be equal to the derivative

01:01:57.840 --> 01:02:02.720
of the Hamiltonian with
respect to the costate vector,

01:02:02.720 --> 01:02:05.080
which is indeed--

01:02:05.080 --> 01:02:10.860
we know already this is our
f, which just to spell it out,

01:02:10.860 --> 01:02:20.000
this essentially means x1 dot
equals x2, and x2 dot equals u.

01:02:20.000 --> 01:02:24.190
So now here I'm omitting
t in between parentheses

01:02:24.190 --> 01:02:25.380
just to save on space.

01:02:25.380 --> 01:02:27.130
But everything here
is a function of time.

01:02:29.650 --> 01:02:33.630
So this is the intuitive
one, just our dynamics.

01:02:33.630 --> 01:02:35.790
Now for the second one.

01:02:35.790 --> 01:02:40.310
For the second one,
what we have is

01:02:40.310 --> 01:02:44.630
p dot is equal to
minus the derivative

01:02:44.630 --> 01:02:53.990
of the Hamiltonian with respect
to x, which is equal to p1 dot.

01:02:53.990 --> 01:02:58.790
So the Hamiltonian is
this guy over here.

01:02:58.790 --> 01:03:03.110
So taking the partial
derivative with respect to x1,

01:03:03.110 --> 01:03:08.350
so there is no x1 here, so
it's just p1 dot equals 0.

01:03:08.350 --> 01:03:23.840
And then P2 DOT in this
case equals negative p1.

01:03:28.300 --> 01:03:28.900
Yes.

01:03:28.900 --> 01:03:34.520
Because we're taking the
derivative with respect to x2.

01:03:34.520 --> 01:03:35.020
Yeah.

01:03:39.500 --> 01:03:42.020
Does this make sense?

01:03:42.020 --> 01:03:45.820
Yeah, perfect.

01:03:45.820 --> 01:03:50.420
Now for the third, what we do
is that we set the derivative

01:03:50.420 --> 01:03:55.060
with respect of the
Hamiltonian in the u, which

01:03:55.060 --> 01:04:08.620
is equal to bu plus p2 from
here, which means that u is

01:04:08.620 --> 01:04:12.380
equal to negative 1 over bp2.

01:04:12.380 --> 01:04:14.740
So this is, for example
to refer to your comment

01:04:14.740 --> 01:04:17.680
before, what I meant
by in the third case,

01:04:17.680 --> 01:04:22.290
we want to express u was
a function of p and x.

01:04:22.290 --> 01:04:23.590
In this case is only p.

01:04:26.890 --> 01:04:32.810
And so from this, if we are
putting these two together,

01:04:32.810 --> 01:04:41.590
what we get is that x.2 is
equal to negative 1 over bp2.

01:04:46.510 --> 01:04:51.510
So just as a caveat, in general,
this is where you would stop.

01:04:51.510 --> 01:04:54.650
So you derive the
optimality conditions.

01:04:54.650 --> 01:04:56.530
You would feed this
to a solver together

01:04:56.530 --> 01:04:59.090
with the appropriate
boundary conditions and hope

01:04:59.090 --> 01:05:01.530
that the solver converges
and you're done.

01:05:01.530 --> 01:05:04.910
Now for the purpose
of being methodical,

01:05:04.910 --> 01:05:07.430
let's try to solve
this analytically.

01:05:30.960 --> 01:05:50.440
So we know from over there that
p1 dot of t is equal to 0--

01:05:50.440 --> 01:05:52.880
so I'm just rewriting here--
which essentially means

01:05:52.880 --> 01:05:58.540
that p1 of t is equal to c1.

01:05:58.540 --> 01:06:01.040
So now we're starting to see
our constants of integration

01:06:01.040 --> 01:06:04.640
starting to pop up.

01:06:04.640 --> 01:06:08.480
So we know from
p2 of t, that has

01:06:08.480 --> 01:06:16.560
to be equal to negative p1 of t,
again from up there, that this

01:06:16.560 --> 01:06:29.830
means that p2 dot is equal to
negative C1, which essentially

01:06:29.830 --> 01:06:41.010
means that p2 of t is equal
to negative c1t plus c2.

01:06:48.510 --> 01:06:55.590
Then again going
forward, we that x2 dot

01:06:55.590 --> 01:07:02.230
is equal to negative
1 over b times p2t.

01:07:02.230 --> 01:07:05.770
Again, we got this
from over here.

01:07:05.770 --> 01:07:07.130
So I'm just rewriting it--

01:07:12.310 --> 01:07:23.200
which is equal to 1 over b
multiply it by minus c1t plus c2

01:07:23.200 --> 01:07:26.860
because this is what
we just derived,

01:07:26.860 --> 01:07:33.220
which essentially means that
integrating we get x of x2 of t

01:07:33.220 --> 01:07:41.900
is equal to negative 1 over b
times minus c1 over 2t squared

01:07:41.900 --> 01:07:45.400
plus c2t plus c3.

01:07:54.580 --> 01:07:57.460
So here to avoid
having to carry over

01:07:57.460 --> 01:08:02.460
this c3 term in the
following computations,

01:08:02.460 --> 01:08:05.860
I'm already exploiting
the fact that we

01:08:05.860 --> 01:08:08.980
know that our initial
conditions are fixed.

01:08:08.980 --> 01:08:15.408
So specifically we know that--

01:08:15.408 --> 01:08:16.450
let's just write it here.

01:08:20.300 --> 01:08:23.590
x dot at time 0 is equal to 0.

01:08:23.590 --> 01:08:29.050
So we start from a 0
velocity condition,

01:08:29.050 --> 01:08:42.729
which means that x20 is
equal to 0, which essentially

01:08:42.729 --> 01:08:48.069
means that, combining these
two, c3 is equal to 0.

01:09:02.370 --> 01:09:03.670
Actually let's go over here.

01:09:06.590 --> 01:09:16.800
Now, we keep going and we have
that x1 dot t is equal to x2t.

01:09:16.800 --> 01:09:19.760
Again, from leveraging our
differential equations right

01:09:19.760 --> 01:09:28.800
there, which is equal to
negative 1 over b times negative

01:09:28.800 --> 01:09:36.620
c1 over 2t squared plus c2t,
where by integrating it,

01:09:36.620 --> 01:09:46.279
it means that x1t is equal to 1,
negative 1 over b times negative

01:09:46.279 --> 01:09:56.580
c1 over 6t power 3 plus
ct times t.2 plus c4.

01:10:05.060 --> 01:10:07.120
So here we essentially
spelled out

01:10:07.120 --> 01:10:11.150
all of our
differential equations.

01:10:11.150 --> 01:10:14.310
And now what we need is we
need our boundary conditions

01:10:14.310 --> 01:10:18.630
to pinpoint all of our
constants of integrations, which

01:10:18.630 --> 01:10:22.150
in this case is c1, c2, c3, c4.

01:10:22.150 --> 01:10:24.630
c3 we already used
it here, but it's

01:10:24.630 --> 01:10:27.470
those 4 plus the final time tf.

01:10:27.470 --> 01:10:30.010
So we need to pinpoint those
five constants of integration.

01:10:30.010 --> 01:10:34.750
And so we need five boundary
conditions to do so.

01:10:34.750 --> 01:10:36.610
So again, what do we know?

01:10:36.610 --> 01:10:42.910
So we know that x1
0 is equal to 10.

01:10:42.910 --> 01:10:46.630
We know that x2 0 is equal to 0.

01:10:46.630 --> 01:10:48.750
So our position at
the initial time is 10

01:10:48.750 --> 01:10:52.390
and we are at 0 velocity.

01:10:52.390 --> 01:10:57.570
We know that x1 at the
final time tf is equal to 0.

01:10:57.570 --> 01:11:00.810
And we have to get
there with 0 velocity.

01:11:00.810 --> 01:11:03.470
And so x2 tf is equal to 0.

01:11:03.470 --> 01:11:06.670
So here we have four
boundary conditions.

01:11:06.670 --> 01:11:13.310
And now to get the fifth, we
essentially look at our table,

01:11:13.310 --> 01:11:19.570
and we look at the case when
the final position is specified.

01:11:19.570 --> 01:11:22.550
So this one here.

01:11:22.550 --> 01:11:26.910
So x of tf is a
specified final state.

01:11:26.910 --> 01:11:29.630
And so this means that
we need to include

01:11:29.630 --> 01:11:34.430
this additional boundary
condition over here.

01:11:34.430 --> 01:11:37.830
So what does that boundary
condition look like in practice,

01:11:37.830 --> 01:11:41.430
is that we need to
take the Hamiltonian,

01:11:41.430 --> 01:11:43.870
evaluate it at final time--

01:11:43.870 --> 01:11:47.350
I'm using this notation to
mean evaluated at final time

01:11:47.350 --> 01:11:52.310
to avoid writing a
sequence of parentheses--

01:11:52.310 --> 01:11:59.590
plus the derivative of the
terminal cost also evaluated

01:11:59.590 --> 01:12:02.370
at the final time
must be equal to 0.

01:12:02.370 --> 01:12:05.270
So this is our fifth
boundary condition

01:12:05.270 --> 01:12:11.220
exactly how we got
it from the table.

01:12:11.220 --> 01:12:13.620
So let's just do that.

01:12:13.620 --> 01:12:27.700
So the Hamiltonian evaluated
at the final time is 1 over 2bu

01:12:27.700 --> 01:12:49.840
squared tf plus p1 tf times x2
at tf plus p2 tf times u tf.

01:12:49.840 --> 01:12:53.180
So here I just took our
expression of the Hamiltonian

01:12:53.180 --> 01:12:57.340
and I just evaluated
at the final time.

01:12:57.340 --> 01:13:05.180
Now we know that
this is equal to 0

01:13:05.180 --> 01:13:14.230
because we know that x2 of
tf is equal to 0 over here.

01:13:19.130 --> 01:13:35.090
And we know that this part over
here is negative b ut from--

01:13:35.090 --> 01:13:35.950
where is it?

01:13:45.210 --> 01:13:46.630
Can you spot it?

01:13:50.330 --> 01:13:52.130
Yeah, from here, for example.

01:13:52.130 --> 01:13:57.030
So we know that p2 evaluated at
tf is equal to negative b ut.

01:13:59.730 --> 01:14:04.490
And so this essentially means
that the Hamiltonian evaluated

01:14:04.490 --> 01:14:08.760
at the final time is
equal to negative 1

01:14:08.760 --> 01:14:13.980
over 2 times bu squared tf.

01:14:16.960 --> 01:14:19.640
So this is the first
of our two terms

01:14:19.640 --> 01:14:21.820
that we are computing over
our boundary condition.

01:14:21.820 --> 01:14:23.480
We just need the
last one, which is

01:14:23.480 --> 01:14:26.200
the derivative of the
terminal cost evaluated

01:14:26.200 --> 01:14:36.140
at the final time, which
is equal to alpha tf.

01:14:36.140 --> 01:14:42.820
So again the terminal cost
for us is this one here,

01:14:42.820 --> 01:14:44.780
so 1/2 alpha tf squared.

01:14:48.800 --> 01:14:50.080
Are we able to see over there?

01:14:50.080 --> 01:14:51.120
I saw you are--

01:14:51.120 --> 01:14:52.200
does this help?

01:14:52.200 --> 01:14:54.600
Yeah.

01:14:54.600 --> 01:14:55.340
All right.

01:14:55.340 --> 01:14:59.760
And so basically summing these
two parts together what we get

01:14:59.760 --> 01:15:13.830
is that 1 over 2 bu
squared tf plus alpha

01:15:13.830 --> 01:15:17.370
tf must be equal to 0.

01:15:17.370 --> 01:15:26.415
So essentially, we got our
five boundary conditions

01:15:26.415 --> 01:15:29.050
to pinpoint our five
constants of integration.

01:15:29.050 --> 01:15:31.310
Yes.

01:15:31.310 --> 01:15:34.430
So it's back to the exponent
question in the top-right

01:15:34.430 --> 01:15:36.883
of the bottom-right corner.

01:15:36.883 --> 01:15:39.090
Should it be [INAUDIBLE]?

01:15:41.710 --> 01:15:44.120
Yes.

01:15:44.120 --> 01:15:46.298
Or is there a reason for it.

01:15:46.298 --> 01:15:48.590
Wait, so you're saying that
this should be 1/2 half or?

01:15:48.590 --> 01:15:51.670
Or be a c2 equation
in the middle of the--

01:15:54.286 --> 01:15:56.350
just like inside the
brackets, the C2.

01:15:56.350 --> 01:15:59.610
c2t squared should
be 1/ct square or--

01:16:03.460 --> 01:16:04.740
on the far right.

01:16:04.740 --> 01:16:06.260
Yeah, I see.

01:16:06.260 --> 01:16:09.320
Yeah, I think you're right.

01:16:09.320 --> 01:16:09.820
Yeah.

01:16:13.220 --> 01:16:14.240
It should be like this.

01:16:14.240 --> 01:16:16.140
Yeah.

01:16:16.140 --> 01:16:17.000
This makes sense.

01:16:20.640 --> 01:16:26.380
And so basically what happens
is that if we-- now we

01:16:26.380 --> 01:16:30.260
have all of the
ingredients to pinpoint

01:16:30.260 --> 01:16:31.905
our constants of integration.

01:16:31.905 --> 01:16:33.780
And what we get is that
the solution actually

01:16:33.780 --> 01:16:36.000
looks like something like this.

01:16:36.000 --> 01:16:38.280
So we can pinpoint our
constants of integration.

01:16:38.280 --> 01:16:41.900
And specifically we have that
the final times looks like 1,800

01:16:41.900 --> 01:16:45.620
times b divided by
alpha power of 1/5.

01:16:45.620 --> 01:16:50.180
Now well, this looks
like an OK beast.

01:16:50.180 --> 01:16:53.280
It kind of aligns
with intuition,

01:16:53.280 --> 01:16:58.500
because so the higher b, so the
higher our weight on the control

01:16:58.500 --> 01:17:03.030
effort the higher
the final time.

01:17:03.030 --> 01:17:04.690
So we'll tend to go slower.

01:17:04.690 --> 01:17:06.330
On the other hand,
the higher alpha,

01:17:06.330 --> 01:17:08.470
the lower will be
the final time.

01:17:08.470 --> 01:17:13.490
So the more will be quicker at
reaching our final condition.

01:17:13.490 --> 01:17:14.330
Yes.

01:17:14.330 --> 01:17:18.610
For this initial condition
given by the table,

01:17:18.610 --> 01:17:21.130
is there any sort of
intuition behind it,

01:17:21.130 --> 01:17:24.640
or should we just pick it
up and just memorize it, not

01:17:24.640 --> 01:17:25.570
worry about it?

01:17:25.570 --> 01:17:27.250
Yeah, so this is
a great question

01:17:27.250 --> 01:17:32.190
because it's a great segue into
the next slide, which is indeed

01:17:32.190 --> 01:17:33.690
trying to give you
some intuition to

01:17:33.690 --> 01:17:37.930
into how those
equations come up.

01:17:37.930 --> 01:17:44.170
Well, to be exact, the boundary
conditions, there are just--

01:17:44.170 --> 01:17:46.090
let's go through this
and then maybe we

01:17:46.090 --> 01:17:49.710
can take this question again.

01:17:49.710 --> 01:17:51.870
So we introduced the
optimality conditions.

01:17:51.870 --> 01:17:53.790
We showed how to use them.

01:17:53.790 --> 01:17:55.250
Now I want to just
spend a couple

01:17:55.250 --> 01:17:57.250
of minutes to giving you
an intuition into how

01:17:57.250 --> 01:17:58.230
those are derived.

01:17:58.230 --> 01:17:59.400
Yes.

01:17:59.400 --> 01:18:03.240
Yeah, so for the final
[INAUDIBLE] final time,

01:18:03.240 --> 01:18:07.803
is there a way to solve for
gold input chemistry or no?

01:18:07.803 --> 01:18:10.380
because you put
that expression tf,

01:18:10.380 --> 01:18:12.160
but you've never solved for--

01:18:12.160 --> 01:18:14.360
No well, of course you
would get also the--

01:18:14.360 --> 01:18:16.900
once you have everything
here from your Solver,

01:18:16.900 --> 01:18:19.640
you would get also kind
of all the triplets.

01:18:19.640 --> 01:18:21.700
You would get x, u and p.

01:18:28.920 --> 01:18:34.040
So remember how we
derived the Euler equation

01:18:34.040 --> 01:18:36.040
in the unconstrained case.

01:18:36.040 --> 01:18:37.680
And specifically
our argument there

01:18:37.680 --> 01:18:40.100
is that we started
from the variation.

01:18:40.100 --> 01:18:42.160
So we took the
fundamental theorem

01:18:42.160 --> 01:18:43.360
of calculus of variation.

01:18:43.360 --> 01:18:49.680
So that the variation
has to be equal to 0.

01:18:49.680 --> 01:18:52.480
And so specifically
we got to here

01:18:52.480 --> 01:18:56.120
by-- so we turned this into
an actionable condition

01:18:56.120 --> 01:18:58.990
by starting from the
increment, taking

01:18:58.990 --> 01:19:01.950
the linear part of
that increment, which

01:19:01.950 --> 01:19:04.790
is equal to the variation.

01:19:04.790 --> 01:19:06.830
And then we derive
that condition

01:19:06.830 --> 01:19:10.130
on the integrand
of that linear part

01:19:10.130 --> 01:19:11.630
by leveraging the
fact that we could

01:19:11.630 --> 01:19:16.670
have any potential
admissible variation delta x.

01:19:16.670 --> 01:19:20.230
So this is just at a high
level what we did on Monday.

01:19:20.230 --> 01:19:22.110
So ideally to derive
those conditions,

01:19:22.110 --> 01:19:24.950
we do a very similar process.

01:19:24.950 --> 01:19:30.350
However now the problem here is
that those variations cannot be

01:19:30.350 --> 01:19:33.030
arbitrary because these are
coupled together through

01:19:33.030 --> 01:19:33.810
the dynamics.

01:19:33.810 --> 01:19:38.110
So the u's and the x's cannot
really vary or arbitrary.

01:19:38.110 --> 01:19:43.630
And so the way we can
derive this is that we--

01:19:43.630 --> 01:19:46.910
first of all here we consider
our simplified setting

01:19:46.910 --> 01:19:50.590
where we set to 0,
the terminal penalty.

01:19:50.590 --> 01:19:55.620
And we assume that the
final time and the x of tf f

01:19:55.620 --> 01:19:57.540
fixed and given.

01:19:57.540 --> 01:20:00.820
And in order to derive
the optimality conditions

01:20:00.820 --> 01:20:06.540
for this case, we consider
the augmented cost function,

01:20:06.540 --> 01:20:10.140
which is basically
what we did before,

01:20:10.140 --> 01:20:18.980
which is essentially a
trick to basically add 0

01:20:18.980 --> 01:20:22.620
to our original cost g.

01:20:22.620 --> 01:20:25.540
The reason I'm saying
that this is a trick

01:20:25.540 --> 01:20:27.720
is because if you look
at this term over here,

01:20:27.720 --> 01:20:31.220
we have sf minus x dot.

01:20:31.220 --> 01:20:34.480
And since this is a
optimality condition,

01:20:34.480 --> 01:20:37.560
in order to be optimal the
point also has to be feasible.

01:20:37.560 --> 01:20:40.180
And so this has
to be equal to 0.

01:20:40.180 --> 01:20:41.780
So essentially, this
is a trick where

01:20:41.780 --> 01:20:47.380
we are defining these augmented
cost by nominally adding 0.

01:20:47.380 --> 01:20:50.820
But we are introducing these
costate variables or the analog

01:20:50.820 --> 01:20:55.860
of the Lagrangian which we will
see later we can then use these

01:20:55.860 --> 01:20:59.140
in order to select those
costate variables in such a way

01:20:59.140 --> 01:21:02.340
to simplify our analysis.

01:21:02.340 --> 01:21:03.060
Yes.

01:21:03.060 --> 01:21:03.940
Sorry.

01:21:03.940 --> 01:21:07.980
On the previous, how did it go
from not knowing u [INAUDIBLE]

01:21:07.980 --> 01:21:09.740
were solving for, leading--

01:21:09.740 --> 01:21:15.380
of that final circle problem to
getting that t is the [? 1v. ?]

01:21:15.380 --> 01:21:17.858
Yes, do you mind if we take
this in a couple of minutes?

01:21:17.858 --> 01:21:19.400
I just want to go
through this slide.

01:21:19.400 --> 01:21:22.060
Yeah, thanks.

01:21:22.060 --> 01:21:24.100
So essentially, we
are in a situation

01:21:24.100 --> 01:21:26.420
where the augmented
cost function is

01:21:26.420 --> 01:21:31.700
something that takes this
augmented g that we just did.

01:21:31.700 --> 01:21:35.220
And so by doing an exactly
the same process as we

01:21:35.220 --> 01:21:37.300
did to derive the
Euler equation,

01:21:37.300 --> 01:21:39.900
we essentially rewrite
the fundamental theorem

01:21:39.900 --> 01:21:44.020
of calculus of variation in
this nasty looking beast.

01:21:44.020 --> 01:21:47.100
But if you think about it,
this is exactly the same thing

01:21:47.100 --> 01:21:49.140
we were doing in
the Euler equation,

01:21:49.140 --> 01:21:53.930
where we had our partial
derivatives of the cost, which

01:21:53.930 --> 01:21:56.850
we obtained by
basically doing a Taylor

01:21:56.850 --> 01:22:00.730
expansion of our increment.

01:22:00.730 --> 01:22:02.570
This term here
corresponds to the term

01:22:02.570 --> 01:22:05.530
where we were using the
integration by part to eliminate

01:22:05.530 --> 01:22:08.930
the dependency on delta x dot.

01:22:08.930 --> 01:22:13.450
And just to make sure that this
is slightly more explicit, so

01:22:13.450 --> 01:22:14.310
this term over here.

01:22:14.310 --> 01:22:17.570
So the derivative of ga
with respect to the costate

01:22:17.570 --> 01:22:23.210
is just the term of our dynamics
which were looking at before.

01:22:23.210 --> 01:22:25.410
This is equal to
negative costate.

01:22:25.410 --> 01:22:29.130
And this is equal to these
two partial derivatives.

01:22:29.130 --> 01:22:31.050
Now the argument
here is that as we

01:22:31.050 --> 01:22:36.690
did before, we want to get
an actionable condition

01:22:36.690 --> 01:22:40.410
by leveraging the fact that the
variations could be arbitrary,

01:22:40.410 --> 01:22:44.050
such that everything inside the
integrand has to be equal to 0

01:22:44.050 --> 01:22:47.370
in order for the entire
integral to be equal to 0.

01:22:47.370 --> 01:22:52.180
The problem here is that
out of all of these terms,

01:22:52.180 --> 01:22:58.200
the only variations that we can
freely vary are the ones in u,

01:22:58.200 --> 01:23:01.600
because everything else is
coupled with respect to you.

01:23:01.600 --> 01:23:04.000
So what we want to
do is that we ideally

01:23:04.000 --> 01:23:06.760
want to remove all
of this extra stuff,

01:23:06.760 --> 01:23:09.000
be left only with
this part here.

01:23:09.000 --> 01:23:12.120
And here we can actually do the
argument that we want to do.

01:23:12.120 --> 01:23:18.360
So the first one is
trivially satisfied

01:23:18.360 --> 01:23:21.880
because for a point to be
optimal it has to be feasible.

01:23:21.880 --> 01:23:24.480
So this part here is actually 0.

01:23:24.480 --> 01:23:26.800
So this term cancels out.

01:23:26.800 --> 01:23:29.400
Now for these two
terms, remember

01:23:29.400 --> 01:23:33.340
that we introduced the
costate arbitrarily.

01:23:33.340 --> 01:23:36.000
So we fictitiously
augmented our cost function

01:23:36.000 --> 01:23:40.160
adding 0 with these
additional costate functions.

01:23:40.160 --> 01:23:43.640
So here we actually could choose
the costate in a, quote unquote,

01:23:43.640 --> 01:23:46.440
"smart way" in order to
cancel everything out.

01:23:46.440 --> 01:23:48.540
And so this is
exactly what we do.

01:23:48.540 --> 01:23:52.030
So we set that p is going to
be equal to this other stuff.

01:23:52.030 --> 01:23:54.630
And so we are left with
only this term where

01:23:54.630 --> 01:23:56.810
the variations are arbitrary.

01:23:56.810 --> 01:23:58.790
And here, we could
do the argument

01:23:58.790 --> 01:24:01.430
that this thing has
to be equal to 0.

01:24:01.430 --> 01:24:07.390
And so if you think back at what
the actual necessary optimality

01:24:07.390 --> 01:24:10.730
conditions were, these were
exactly those conditions.

01:24:10.730 --> 01:24:13.830
So remember we had three
two differential equations

01:24:13.830 --> 01:24:15.570
and some algebraic equations.

01:24:15.570 --> 01:24:18.090
The first one was that the
dynamics need to be satisfied.

01:24:18.090 --> 01:24:19.830
And it's this term over here.

01:24:19.830 --> 01:24:23.130
The second one was a condition
on the costate variables,

01:24:23.130 --> 01:24:25.270
and it's this term over here.

01:24:25.270 --> 01:24:28.310
And then the last one was
that this term over here

01:24:28.310 --> 01:24:30.630
needs to be equal to 0.

01:24:30.630 --> 01:24:33.777
So I invite you to do the
mapping between these slides

01:24:33.777 --> 01:24:36.110
and the actual formulation,
where the only difference is

01:24:36.110 --> 01:24:41.070
that we're being more elegant
by defining the Hamiltonian.

01:24:41.070 --> 01:24:42.530
And this is all for today.

01:24:42.530 --> 01:24:44.080
Thanks.

